{
  "dom_id": "step-q114270",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
